{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 879 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "cb438bf0f40639d5c94fd750ea3fd140e9066cacd31dd8dc777687118f979e01" :edb/db-before-t 878 :edb/db-after-t 879 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534191 50 #inst "2026-10-11T17:35:55.828Z" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186151840 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the residue of $76 \\cdot 122 \\textrm{ mod } 6.$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186151840 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the residue of $76 \\cdot 122 \\textrm{mod} 6$." 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186151840 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n$76\\begin{vmatrix}≡12 \\cdot 6 + 4 & (mod6) \\\\ ≡12 \\cdot 0 + 4 & (mod6) \\\\ ≡4 & (mod6) \\\\ 122 & ≡20 \\cdot 6 + 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ≡20 \\cdot 0 + 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod6) \\\\ ≡2\\end{vmatrix}(mod6)$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n76 \\cdot 122 \\mid ≡4 \\cdot 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod6) \\\\ ≡8\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ≡6 + 2\\begin{vmatrix}(mod6) \\\\ ≡0 + 2\\end{vmatrix}(mod6); ≡2 \\mid (mod6)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n76 \\cdot 122 \\equiv 2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186151840 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}76 & ≡12\\cdot 6+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡12\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 122 & ≡20\\cdot 6+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡20\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}76\\cdot 122 & ≡4\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡6+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n76 \\cdot 122 \\equiv 2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6).\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186151841 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the residue of $5^{7} \\textrm{ mod } 8.$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186151841 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the residue of $5^{7} \\textrm{mod} 8$." 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186151841 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n5^{7} = 5^2\\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 5^3\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n5^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}≡25 & (mod8) \\\\ ≡3 \\cdot 8 + 1 & (mod8) \\\\ ≡3 \\cdot 0 + 1 & (mod8) \\\\ ≡1 & (mod8) \\\\ 5^{3} & ≡5 \\cdot 5^{2}\\end{vmatrix}(mod8); ≡5 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}(mod8) \\\\ ≡5\\end{vmatrix}(mod8)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n5^{7} \\mid ≡5^{2} \\cdot 5^{2} \\cdot 5^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}(mod8) \\\\ ≡1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 5\\end{vmatrix}(mod8); ≡5 \\mid (mod8)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5^{7} \\textrm{mod} 8= 5\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186151841 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n5^{7} = 5^2\\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 5^3.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & ≡25\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 8+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ {5}^{3} & ≡5\\cdot {5}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡5\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{7} & ≡{5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 1\\cdot 5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5^{7} \\textrm{ mod } 8= 5.\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186151856 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $3x \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$?\n\n1. $x \\equiv 5 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$\n2. $x \\equiv 7 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$\n3. $x \\equiv 9 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186151856 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $3x \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)?$ \n\n- $x  \\equiv 5 \\quad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $x  \\equiv 7 \\quad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $x  \\equiv 9 \\quad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186151856 1074 "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, we have the following:\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nThe modulus equals $20$ in all statements, which suggests we'll need to use the first property.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $3$, and\n\n$$\n7\\cdot 3 = 21\n$$\n\nis one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x & ≡1 & (mod20) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 3x & ≡7 \\cdot 1 & (mod20) \\\\ 21x & ≡7 & (mod20) \\\\ x + 20x & ≡7 & (mod20) \\\\ x + 0x & ≡7 & (mod20) \\\\ x & ≡7 & (mod20)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$3x \\equiv 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x \\equiv 7\\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186151856 1074 "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ we have the following:\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nThe modulus equals $20$ in all statements, which suggests we'll need to use the first property.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $3,$ and $7\\cdot 3 = 21$ is one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 7\\cdot 3x & ≡7\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 21x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+20x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+0x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n3x \\equiv 1  \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \nx \\equiv 7\\qquad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186151864 1071 "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of $17^{13}$?" 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186151864 1071 "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of $17^{13}?$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186151864 1074 "If $a\\equiv b\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c\\equiv d\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$, then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac\\equiv bd \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n- $a^k \\equiv b^k \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n$17^{13} \\textrm{mod} 10$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n17 \\mid ≡10 + 7\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡0 + 7\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡7 \\mid (mod10)\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n17^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}≡7^{2} & (mod10) \\\\ ≡49 & (mod10) \\\\ ≡4 \\cdot 10 + 9 & (mod10) \\\\ ≡4 \\cdot 0 + 9 & (mod10) \\\\ ≡9 & (mod10) \\\\ 17^{4} & ≡9^{2}\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡81\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡8 \\cdot 10 + 1\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡8 \\cdot 0 + 1\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡1\\end{vmatrix}(mod10)\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose $17^{13}$ into a product of smaller exponents.\n\n$$\n17^{13} = \\left(17^4\\right)^3\\cdot 17\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\mid ≡(17^{4})^{3} \\cdot 17\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡(1)^{3} \\cdot 7\\end{vmatrix}(mod10); ≡1 \\cdot 7\\begin{vmatrix}(mod10) \\\\ ≡7\\end{vmatrix}(mod10)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\textrm{mod} 10 = 7\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of $17^{13}$ is $7$." 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186151864 1074 "If $a\\equiv b\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c\\equiv d\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac\\equiv bd  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a^k \\equiv b^k  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\textrm{ mod } 10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17 & ≡10+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{17}^{2} & ≡{7}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡49\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ {17}^{4} & ≡{9}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 10+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose $17^{13}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n17^{13} =  \\left(17^4\\right)^3\\cdot 17\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{17}^{13} & ≡{({17}^{4})}^{3}\\cdot 17\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(1)^{3}\\cdot 7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\textrm{ mod } 10 = 7\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of $17^{13}$ is $7.$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413569 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413571 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413576 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413578 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413586 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413570 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413573 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413574 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152091 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413585 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413579 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413581 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413582 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413583 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152092 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413584 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413572 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413575 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413577 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152093 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413580 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152341 1050 780.0 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149472 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186152483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413568 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152483 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$84\\cdot 102\\text{ mod }5=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152483 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.$84 \\cdot 102mod5 =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186152483 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}84 & ≡16\\cdot 5+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡16\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 102 & ≡20\\cdot 5+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡20\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}84\\cdot 102 & ≡4\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡5+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n84\\cdot 102\\text{ mod }5=3.\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149485 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186152486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413557 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152486 1071 "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of $19^{19}$?" 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186152486 1071 "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${19}^{19}?$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186152486 1074 "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{19}^{19}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19 & ≡10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{19}^{2} & ≡{9}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 10+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${19}^{19}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{19}^{19}={({19}^{2})}^{9}\\cdot 19\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{19}^{19} & ≡{({19}^{2})}^{9}\\cdot 19\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(1)^{9}\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{19}^{19}\\text{ mod }10=9\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${19}^{19}$ is $9.$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186153451 1064 "Let's consider the modular congruence\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nOne useful property of congruences is that we can *multiply* both sides by an *integer*, similar to regular equations.\n\nFor example, consider the true statement\n\n$$\n7\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5)\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by (for example) $9$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\cdot 7 & ≡9 \\cdot 2 & (mod5) \\\\ 63 & ≡18 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement since $63$ and $18$ both have a remainder of $3$ when divided by $5$.\n\nIn general, the **scalar multiplication property** of congruences states that for $k\\in\\mathbb{Z}$,\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186153451 1064 "Let's consider the modular congruence\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nOne useful property of congruences is that we can *multiply* both sides by an *integer*, similar to regular equations.\n\nFor example, consider the true statement\n\n$$\n7\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by (for example) $9$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot 7 & ≡9\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 63 & ≡18\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement since $63$ and $18$ both have a remainder of $3$ when divided by $5.$\n\nIn general, the **scalar multiplication property** of congruences states that for $k\\in\\mathbb{Z},$\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn).\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186153463 1064 "Another useful property that stems from the multiplication property is as follows. Suppose that\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nthen, we have\n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nUsing this, we can compute the residue of a large product without finding the product explicitly. Let's see how." 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186153463 1064 "Another useful property that stems from the multiplication property is as follows. Suppose that \n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nUsing this, we can compute the residue of a large product without finding the product explicitly. Let's see how." 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186153469 1064 "The multiplicative property states that given $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c \\equiv d \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$, we obtain $ac \\equiv bd \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$. By taking the same congruence twice, we obtain\n\n$$\na^2 \\equiv b^2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\na^k \\equiv b^k \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nfor any *positive* integer $k$." 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186153469 1064 "The multiplicative property states that given $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c \\equiv d \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),$ we obtain $ac \\equiv bd \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).$ By taking the same congruence twice, we obtain \n\n$$\na^2 \\equiv b^2  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\na^k \\equiv b^k  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nfor any *positive* integer $k.$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186153473 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we've made use of the following properties:\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nLet's prove the first property. The proof of the second property is similar.\n\nIt's straightforward to prove the scalar multiplication property from the definition of congruence. First, note that we have the following chain of implications for $k, p\\in\\mathbb Z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\, & ⟹\\,a-b=pn \\\\  & ⟹\\,k(a-b)=kpn \\\\  & ⟹\\,ka-kb=(kp)n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $kp = q\\in\\mathbb Z.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka-kb=(kp)n\\, & ⟹\\,ka-kb=qn \\\\  & ⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The proof of the second property is very similar.\n\n- We'll prove the multiplicative and exponentiation properties in future lessons." 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186153473 1064 "Throughout this lesson, we've made use of the following properties:\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nLet's prove the first property. The proof of the second property is similar.\n\nIt's straightforward to prove the scalar multiplication property from the definition of congruence. First, note that we have the following chain of implications for $k, p\\in\\mathbb Z$:\n\n$$\na≡b(modn) \\mid ⟹a - b = pn; ⟹k(a - b) = kpn; ⟹ka - kb = (kp)n\n$$\n\nNow, let\n\n$$\nkp = q\\in\\mathbb Z\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nka - kb = (kp)n \\mid ⟹ka - kb = qn; ⟹ka≡kb(modn)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The proof of the second property is very similar.\n- We'll prove the multiplicative and exponentiation properties in future lessons." 13194139534191 17592186045417 false] [17592186413465 1080 "3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413465 1082 #