od}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${12}^{17}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{12}^{17}={({12}^{8})}^{2}\\cdot 12\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{12}^{17} & ≡{({12}^{8})}^{2}\\cdot 12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(6)^{2}\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡36\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡72\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 10+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{12}^{17}\\text{ mod }10=2\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${12}^{17}$ is $2.$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413548 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413573 1069 "q-213206" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413573 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$113\\cdot 119\\text{ mod }10=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413573 1072 #uuid "4a7e58ae-7402-5947-902d-187649afe03f" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413573 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}113 & ≡11\\cdot 10+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡11\\cdot 0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 119 & ≡11\\cdot 10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡11\\cdot 0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}113\\cdot 119 & ≡3\\cdot 9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡27\\, & (\\text{mod}\\