y.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $2,$ and $8\\cdot 2=16$ is one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 8\\cdot 2x & ≡8\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 16x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x+15x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x+0x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413554 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413579 1069 "q-213071" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413579 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n${8}^{6}\\text{ mod }9=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413579 1072 #uuid "a3bd2c71-c0a1-5bcf-99be-f98d89cf7b38" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413579 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{8}^{6}={8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2} & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 9+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{6} & ≡{8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{8}^{6}\\,\\,\\text{mod}\\,9=1.\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186