9 "q-213056" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413581 1071 "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${12}^{6}≡$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413581 1072 #uuid "ca2a2b4b-a4c2-5e49-b032-7cf6b84669a2" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413581 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{12}^{6}=(3\\cdot 4)^{6}={3}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3} & ≡27\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 9+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 9+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{12}^{6} & ≡{3}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot 0\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{12}^{6}≡0\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413556 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413582 1069 "q-213048" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413582 1071 "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${6}^{6}≡$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413582 1072 #uuid "cc39a9be-d29f-5f9f-ba0f-0e6107657cab" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413582 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{6}^{6}={6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{2} & ≡36\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 8+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{6} & ≡{6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 4\\cdot 4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 8+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{6}^{6}≡0\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8).\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413550 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413583 1069 "q-213057" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413583 1071 "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${15}^{4}≡$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413583 1072 #uuid "d32d1e8e-c6cb-5e8e-ad44-72ef38726d73" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413583 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{15}^{4}=(3\\cdot 5)^{4}={3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 7+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ {5}^{2} & ≡25\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 7+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{15}^{4} & ≡{3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 2\\cdot 4\\cdot 4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\cdot 7+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{15}^{4}≡1\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413547 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413584 1069 "q-213055" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413584 1071 "By the multiplicative property of congruences, ${20}^{4}≡$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413584 1072 #uuid "d8e0aa45-c7f0-5602-aeab-6e88c994c623" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413584 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{20}^{4}=(4\\cdot 5)^{4}={4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 6+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ {5}^{2} & ≡25\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 6+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{20}^{4} & ≡{4}^{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡16\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 6+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{20}^{4}≡4\\,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413558 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413585 1069 "q-213066" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413585 1071 "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n$94\\cdot 74\\text{ mod }8=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413585 1072 #uuid "e40b6e95-46c9-501b-9cf3-a3ca43d4de46" 13194139534191 17592186045417 true] [17592186413585 1074 "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}94 & ≡11\\cdot 8+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡11\\cdot 0+6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ 74 & ≡9\\cdot 8+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡9\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}94\\cdot 74 & ≡6\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡8+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n94\\cdot 74\\text{ mod